From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan E <76752281+ThaRae@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 16:46:41 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] LibWeb: Add fingerprinting protection by farbling

Sites that fingerprint read values that differ between machines (canvas
pixels, audio samples, the processor count, the amount of memory) and
combine them into an ID that outlives cookies. Following the design of
Brave's "farbling", which this independently reimplements, those values
are now randomized slightly, per site and per session: each site sees
the same values for the whole session, two sites see unrelated values,
and a restart changes all of them.

Web::Fingerprinting mixes a secret with the host of the origin asking.
The secret is LoaderConfig::fingerprinting_session_secret, which the
browser is to share between all of its processes, so that a site can't
open several and combine their readings. Until it's set, each process
makes up its own from the system's random source. On top of that seed:

- Canvas serialization (toDataURL, toBlob, convertToBlob) and
  getImageData return a copy with the low bit of a color channel
  flipped on a scattering of pixels. Which ones depends on the pixels
  too, so a script can't learn the pattern from an image it drew and
  undo it on another. The canvas itself is untouched.
- AudioBuffer channel data and the AnalyserNode getters are scaled by
  a factor in [0.99, 1).
- navigator.hardwareConcurrency is between 4 and min(8, real count),
  except in WebKit mode, whose count is already capped to 4 or 8. And
  navigator.deviceMemory is one of 1, 2, 4, never above the real value.

LoaderConfig::enable_fingerprinting_protection turns it on, and it is
on by default. Test mode turns it off in WebContent and WebWorker,
since test output has to be deterministic.
